@node splash
@section @command{fairing splash}

@cindex splash
@example
fairing splash --stage initrd|system [--theme @var{file.fairing}] [--palette @var{slug}]
@end example

The boot splash itself.  The systemd units run it, once per boot stage
(@pxref{Running under systemd}); a person runs it only to try a theme, and
@command{fairing preview} is the better tool for that.

@table @code
@item --stage initrd
From early boot until switch-root.  The bar follows elapsed time against the
initrd's duration on the previous boot, up to 30 percent; on a first boot it
follows a fixed curve that reaches 30 percent at 8 seconds.  On
@code{SIGTERM} it writes the bar value to @file{/run/fairing/state} for the
next stage and exits.
@item --stage system
From switch-root until greetd starts.  The bar starts from the value the
initrd left and follows systemd's own job-queue progress
(@code{org.freedesktop.systemd1.Manager.Progress}) from 30 to 99 percent; it
reaches 100 percent only at the handoff.  Under the bar it shows the
description of the newest running systemd job.  On @code{SIGTERM} it shows the
full bar, releases the display within 100 ms, writes the measured stage
durations to @file{/var/lib/fairing/boot-duration} and exits.
@item --theme
A compiled theme (@pxref{Themes}).  Without it the built-in theme is drawn.
@item --palette
A registered palette slug, as for @command{fairing preview}.
@end table

@cindex fail-open
The bar never moves backwards: when a new reading is lower it holds, and a
higher one is approached smoothly.  A splash never fails the boot.  Every way
out exits with status 0 and the reason in the report: @code{handoff},
@code{switch-root}, @code{shutdown}, @code{failed-unit} (a systemd unit
failed), @code{maintenance} (rescue or emergency mode started),
@code{no-backend}, @code{render-failed} or @code{theme-unreadable}.  When it
draws nothing it still tells systemd it is ready, so the unit never delays
the boot.

@cindex journal
Under systemd, diagnostics go to the journal as one line each, with a syslog
priority: a missing display and a missing D-Bus connection are
@code{notice}, other trouble is @code{warning}.  The report goes to the
journal too, as one JSON document.

@node theme
@section @command{fairing theme}

@cindex theme check
@cindex theme compile
@cindex theme inspect
@example
fairing theme check @var{theme.ncl}
fairing theme compile @var{theme.ncl} [--output @var{file.fairing}]
fairing theme inspect @var{file.fairing}
@end example

@table @code
@item check
Evaluates the theme against the contract and applies every rule of
@code{compile}, writing nothing.  A violation exits 2 and carries Nickel's own
diagnostic, as plain text, in the error's @code{detail}.
@item compile
Writes the artefact the splash loads, by default beside the source with
@file{.fairing} in place of @file{.ncl}.  The report gives the size and the
median time this machine takes to load the artefact.  @code{--dry-run}
compiles and reports without writing.
@item inspect
Reads an artefact and shows its layouts and images.
@end table

@code{check} and @code{compile} need the Nickel evaluator, which only the
full build carries.  The builds the NixOS module runs at boot do not, and
answer @code{FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE}; they load compiled themes only.

@node preview
@section @command{fairing preview}

@cindex preview
@cindex simulated bar
@example
fairing preview [--seconds @var{n}] [--backend auto|drm|fbdev|memory]
                [--snapshot @var{file.ppm}] [--palette @var{slug}]
                [--status @var{text}] [--size @var{W}x@var{H}] [--fps @var{hz}]
                [--theme @var{file}]
@end example

Draws the splash with a bar that advances from 0 to 100 percent over
@var{n} seconds (default 5; fractions are accepted and @code{0} draws a single
frame), then restores the console.  Theme authors use it to iterate without
rebooting.  The same compositor and backends serve the boot splash.

@table @code
@item --backend
@code{auto} tries DRM/KMS first and falls back to @file{/dev/fb0}; the other
values try one backend only.  @code{memory} renders off-screen and is the only
backend an agent environment may use (@pxref{Environment}).
@item --snapshot
Writes the final frame as a binary PPM (@code{P6}, 8 bits per channel) to the
given path.  The frame is written to a sibling @file{.part} file and renamed
into place once complete, so a failed run leaves an existing file untouched.
The format needs no library and every image viewer opens it.
@item --palette
A registered Steelbore theme slug such as @code{steelbore},
@code{steelbore-high-contrast} or @code{tokyonight}: the explicit source of
the Steelbore Standard §11.6 resolution, reported as @code{command-line}.  It
outranks @env{SPACECRAFT_THEME}; an unregistered slug is a usage error.
Without it the palette is resolved as §11.6 prescribes: the environment
variable, then the family default, with @env{NO_COLOR} (or @code{--no-color})
overlaying the mono theme.  A @code{-high-contrast} or mono slug named here or
in @env{SPACECRAFT_THEME} is pinned and no overlay changes it.  The theme is
resolved once and held for the run.
@item --status
The status line drawn under the bar; the boot splash shows the description of
the systemd job in progress here.
@item --size
The frame size for the memory backend (default @code{1920x1080}), each side
@code{1} to @code{16384} and the whole frame at most 256 MiB.  A device
backend always draws at the connector's preferred mode.
@item --fps
The redraw rate, 1 to 240 frames per second (default 30).
@item --theme
A compiled theme, or a Nickel source that is compiled in memory first (full
build only).  Without it the built-in theme is drawn.
@item --dry-run
Reports which backends would be tried, in order, with which palette, without
touching a device.  A device's mode is not guessed: @code{width},
@code{height} and @code{format} are null in the plan.  The envelope carries
@code{"dry_run": true}.
@end table

@cindex layout
The layout is authored in a 1920x1080 reference frame and scaled
uniformly into the active mode, centred, with the uncovered bands painted in
the canvas colour.  The percentage is always rendered as text beside the bar,
so the state is never carried by colour alone.

@cindex report
In machine mode the @code{data} object reports @code{backend} (@code{auto}
only in a plan, where the chain decides at run time), the @code{chain} tried
in order, @code{device}, @code{width}, @code{height}, @code{format}, the
resolved @code{palette}
(@code{slug}, @code{base}, @code{source}, @code{overlay}, @code{skipped}),
@code{seconds}, @code{fps}, @code{frames}, @code{dropped},
@code{first_frame_ms}, @code{measured_fps}, @code{snapshot}, the
@code{fallbacks} that were tried before the working backend, and
@code{planned}.

@cindex console, restoring
On the DRM path the CRTC is returned to the framebuffer it scanned out before;
on the fbdev path the device contents are saved on open and written back on
close, and the console cursor blink is paused meanwhile.  Both restorations run
on every exit path, including a failure midway through the run.  Both paths
need the process to be the DRM master or to own the framebuffer: run on a text
console, not under a desktop session.  A snapshot path is opened before any
device, so a bad path fails without switching the console.

@node describe and schema
@section @command{fairing describe} and @command{fairing schema}

@cindex describe
@cindex schema
@command{fairing describe} emits the capability manifest: every verb with its
idempotency, destructiveness and dry-run support, the global flags, the output
formats, and the detected reader profile.  It has no side effects.
@command{fairing schema} with no argument emits the schema of the whole tool;
with a verb name it emits that verb's parameters, output schema, exit codes and
examples, all derived from the same argument parser that runs the verb, so the
two cannot drift.

@node Global flags
@section Global flags

@cindex flags, global
@table @code
@item --json
@itemx --format human|json|jsonl|yaml|csv|explore
Output mode.  @code{yaml}, @code{csv} and @code{explore} are accepted and
answered with @code{FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE}, or with a @code{TUI_FALLBACK} warning
plus JSON for @code{explore}.
@item --fields @var{f1,f2,...}
Keep only the named top-level keys of @code{data}.
@item --dry-run
Report the plan without side effects, where the verb supports it.
@item --verbose
@itemx --quiet
Lower the diagnostic floor to @code{info}, or raise it to @code{error}.
Mutually exclusive.
@item --no-color
@itemx --color auto|always|never
Colour policy for human mode.
@item --absolute-time
Timestamps as ISO 8601 UTC in human mode (the only form Fairing uses anyway).
@item --print0
NUL-delimited output for @command{xargs -0}.
@item --yes
@itemx --force
Skip confirmations.
@item --version
Name, version, maintainer, website and copyright, as text or as an envelope.
@end table

@node Environment
@section Environment

@cindex environment variables
@cindex agents
@table @env
@item AI_AGENT
@itemx AGENT
Set to any non-empty value other than @code{0} or @code{false}: machine
output, no colour, no prompts, no virtual terminal, diagnostic floor
@code{warn}.
@item CI
Truthy: machine output, no colour, no virtual terminal.
@item CLAUDECODE
@itemx CURSOR_AGENT
@itemx GEMINI_CLI
Informational: recorded as @code{metadata.tool_agent}; colour, prompts and
the virtual terminal are off, the output format is unchanged.
@item NO_COLOR
@itemx FORCE_COLOR
@itemx CLICOLOR
@itemx TERM
Colour precedence as the CLI Standard defines it.  @env{NO_COLOR} also selects
the mono theme for the splash itself.
@item SPACECRAFT_THEME
A registered theme slug for the splash palette (Steelbore Standard §11.6).
@item NOTIFY_SOCKET
Set by systemd for a @code{Type=notify} unit: @command{fairing splash} sends
@code{READY=1} there after its first frame and @code{STOPPING=1} as it exits.
@item JOURNAL_STREAM
Set by systemd when standard error is the journal: diagnostics are then
written as journal lines with a syslog priority.
@end table

@cindex kernel command line
The splash also reads one kernel parameter, @code{fairing.theme=@var{slug}},
which chooses the palette below @code{--palette} and above
@env{SPACECRAFT_THEME}.

@node Exit codes
@section Exit codes

@cindex exit codes
@multitable @columnfractions .1 .3 .6
@headitem Code @tab Error codes @tab Meaning
@item 0 @tab @tab Success.
@item 1 @tab @code{FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE}, @code{INTERNAL_ERROR}
@tab Not built into this release, or an unexpected failure.
@item 2 @tab @code{INVALID_ARGUMENT}, @code{MISSING_ARGUMENT}
@tab Usage error, including a device backend requested under an agent variable.
@item 3 @tab @code{NOT_FOUND}
@tab No DRM device or @file{/dev/fb0} to draw on, a snapshot path in a
directory that does not exist, or a @command{schema} target that is not a verb.
@item 4 @tab @code{PERMISSION_DENIED}
@tab The device refused the process: not in the @code{video} group, or no
@code{CAP_SYS_ADMIN} to become DRM master.
@item 5 @tab @code{CONFLICT}
@tab Another client holds DRM master, such as a running compositor; stop it or
run on a text console.
@end multitable

Every non-zero exit in machine mode writes one line
@code{@{"error":@{code,exit_code,message,hint,timestamp,command@}@}} to
standard error; @code{hint} is a runnable command.  An error may also carry
@code{detail}, text another tool produced verbatim, such as Nickel's
diagnostic for a theme that breaks the contract.

@command{fairing splash} is the exception: it exits 0 in every case it can
reach, so a splash can never be the reason a boot fails.  Its arguments are
still checked, and a usage error is still exit 2.

@node Themes
@chapter Themes

@cindex theme
@cindex Nickel
@cindex contract
A theme is a Nickel file that satisfies the contract
@file{contracts/fairing-theme.ncl}.  Fairing applies the contract itself, so
the theme need not import it; importing it as well lets an editor check the
file as it is written.  The reference theme, @file{themes/steelbore.ncl}, is
the built-in layout written out in full and is the place to start.

@cindex reference frame
All geometry is in the 1920x1080 reference frame and is scaled uniformly to
the active mode at boot.  A theme declares:

@table @code
@item name
Shown in reports.
@item palette
The registered Steelbore palette the theme is drawn in, such as
@code{steelbore} or @code{tokyonight}.  It is the theme's default: the palette
can still be chosen at boot (@pxref{Palette selection}).
@item layouts.boot
The logo box, the bar (box, corner radius, outline, and the colours of fill,
track and border), the percentage (gap and size), the status line (whether it
is shown, its top and size), the prompt region, and optionally a frame
sequence.
@item layouts.shutdown
The logo, a status line and its message, and optionally a frame sequence.
@end table

@cindex colour
@cindex role
Colours are never written as values.  A colour is a Steelbore role such as
@code{'accent} or @code{'foreground}, or the name of a token of the theme's
own palette, @code{@{ token = "Plasma Orange" @}}.  @command{fairing theme
compile} refuses a literal colour anywhere in the theme, a token of any other
palette, text drawn in a surface colour, and a bar whose fill does not reach
3:1 contrast with its track.  Because the compiled theme stores roles, not
colours, the same artefact draws the high-contrast and mono variants of its
palette without any extra file.

@cindex logo
@cindex frame sequence
A logo is @code{'builtin}, Fairing's vector mark, or a PNG file named
relative to the theme.  Every opaque pixel of a PNG must be a colour of the
declared palette, unless the logo sets @code{tint}, in which case only the
image's alpha channel is used and drawn in that colour.  A frame sequence is
a list of PNG frames of one size, played at up to 30 frames per second, under
the same rule.  Sequences are compiled and stored now; playing them arrives
with the reduced-motion work of a later milestone.

@cindex artefact
The compiled artefact is one file of at most 4 MiB: an eight-byte magic,
a format version, the layouts in postcard encoding, and the images as palette
role indices or alpha masks.  It loads in well under 5 ms.  A version the
running splash does not know is refused, and the splash then steps aside
rather than draw a wrong theme.

@node Palette selection
@section Palette selection

@cindex palette, selection
@cindex Steelbore Standard §11.6
The splash chooses its palette in two stages.  The base palette is the first
registered slug among: @code{--palette} (the NixOS module's @code{palette}
option), the kernel parameter @code{fairing.theme=}, @env{SPACECRAFT_THEME},
the theme's declared @code{palette}, and the family default
@code{steelbore}.  An unregistered slug is skipped, never fatal.  Then,
unless a @code{-high-contrast} or mono slug was named outright, @env{NO_COLOR}
selects the mono variant.  The accessible-mode overlay to
@code{-high-contrast} arrives with the accessibility milestone.

@node Running under systemd
@chapter Running under systemd

@cindex systemd units
@cindex NixOS module
On Steelbore OS Bravais the splash is enabled with one option of the NixOS
module @code{steelbore.fairing}, exported by the repository's flake as
@code{nixosModules.fairing}:

@example
steelbore.fairing = @{
  enable = true;
  kmsModules = [ "i915" ];          # the machine's KMS driver
  # theme = ./my-theme/theme.ncl;   # default: the reference theme
  # palette = "steelbore-high-contrast";
@};
@end example

Enabling it compiles the theme at build time, copies the artefact and a small
build of Fairing into the systemd initrd, adds @code{simpledrm} and the
listed KMS drivers to the initrd, adds @code{quiet} and @code{splash} to the
kernel command line, and installs two units.  The module requires the systemd
initrd and greetd, and refuses a configuration that also enables Plymouth.

@cindex fairing-initrd.service
@table @code
@item fairing-initrd.service
Runs @code{fairing splash --stage initrd} in the initrd, after the kernel
modules are loaded and udev has been triggered.  It has no default
dependencies and is only wanted by @code{initrd.target}.  It is stopped
before switch-root, and leaves @file{/run/fairing} behind for the next stage.
@item fairing.service
Runs @code{fairing splash --stage system} after switch-root, early in
@code{sysinit.target}, and only when @file{/run/fairing} exists: a restart of
the boot targets on a running system, as @command{nixos-rebuild switch} does,
never brings the splash back over a session.  It runs with least privilege:
only the capabilities to hold the display and the console, the file system
read-only except its own two directories, and no network.
@end table

@cindex greetd
@cindex handoff
The handoff to greetd is ordering plus one stop.  @code{fairing.service} is
ordered before @code{greetd.service}, and greetd's start first stops
@code{fairing.service} and waits for it, so the splash has drawn the full bar
and released the display before greetd opens the console.  The two units do
not conflict: in one boot transaction a conflict makes systemd drop greetd's
start job, and the machine would come up without a login screen.

@cindex fail-open
Neither unit is required by any target; a splash that fails, hangs or is
absent cannot stop a boot.  A password question (for example an encrypted
volume) stops the splash so the console prompt is visible; the in-splash
prompt replaces this with a later milestone.  The splash's own waits are all
bounded: five seconds to start, one second to stop.

@cindex D-Bus
In the initrd there is no bus, and that build of Fairing carries no D-Bus
client.  After switch-root the splash connects to the system bus; if no
connection is made within 2 seconds, it says so once in the journal and
drives the bar from the previous boot's measured duration instead.

